From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Fri, 16 Sep 1994 16:21:49 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-188] Networking in the Mind Age

Thanks to Alexander Chislenko for his interesting essay.  Sasha argues
that "life always starts as a set of non-distributed objects" but
"Later ... the evolutionary frontier gradually shifts towards
distributed systems" approaching "direct sharing of internal features
with all interested parties" so that when "one infomorph wants to
learn something from another, it can just copy the necessary
information or access the teacher's knowledge as its own." So "Rather
than independent entities, robots and other smart machines would work
more like semi-intelligent, semi-autonomous front-ends/ clients of the
global system. ... The local machine itself would only store the
knowledge that is frequently used or may be urgently needed." 

Why does Sasha expect such a trend?  First he sees it happening so
far, with the human innovation of cultural evolution: "Humans obtain
most of their knowledge by learning from experience and conclusions of
others".  Second, this seems much more efficient to him: "elimination
of redundant computations within millions of robots would make a
networked system greatly more efficient than a collection of
unconnected machines".  And third, it doesn't seem that hard to Sasha:
"Most of the main networking components of this system  have already
been designed  or at least conceived."

If this is so efficient, why hasn't it happened already?  Sasha blames
human "poor memory, low communication speeds and inability to transfer
knowledge directly" and the fact that "young Nature couldn't develop
information coding and transfer standards at the initial stages of
growth. ... By the time they started accumulating any features worth
sharing, it was too late to change the design."

Now I certainly grant that if robots have cheaper I/O channels than
humans, they will talk more to each other.  But I don't yet buy the
inevitability of standards so strong that all knowledge will be this
easily transferrable, that CPU and bit-sending costs would so dominate
the costs of figuring out what the hell some distantly obtained
"knowledge" means, and of deciding whether it can be trusted.  It
seems that somewhat older Nature could easily repeat the experience of
young Nature, with divergent local evolution preventing total global
integration of knowledge.  There are many great costs, as well as
benefits, to attempting such integration.

Robin Hanson

