From: rjc@gnu.ai.mit.edu (Ray)
Date: Fri, 16 Sep 94 20:40:02 WET DST
Subject: [#94-9-189] Networking in the Mind Age

Robin Hanson writes:
> Now I certainly grant that if robots have cheaper I/O channels than
> humans, they will talk more to each other.  But I don't yet buy the
> inevitability of standards so strong that all knowledge will be this
> easily transferrable, that CPU and bit-sending costs would so dominate
> the costs of figuring out what the hell some distantly obtained
> "knowledge" means, and of deciding whether it can be trusted.  It
> seems that somewhat older Nature could easily repeat the experience of
> young Nature, with divergent local evolution preventing total global
> integration of knowledge.  There are many great costs, as well as
> benefits, to attempting such integration.

   Furthermore, there really isn't a best way to represent knowledge.
It all depends on what you're trying to do and the context of your
goals. Consider the differences between the way someone in the
humanities and someone in the sciences look at a particular
branch of scientific knowledge. I claim that the two people need
to represent knowledge in different ways. For instance, isn't a
certain amount of ignorance (or abstraction) beneficial? If everyone
were to simply access a "storehouse" of knowledge, and that knowledge
was stored in one standard format, there would be little debate
or memetic mutation of that knowledge. The robots would
trust the knowledge and use it without question and since they
have complete knowledge of it. On the other hand, being
abstracted from the knowledge leads one to question it, modify it,
reinterpret it, and inevitably, increase diversity. Back to
the hypothetical humanities infomorph. Just allowing him to access
directly the knowledge and experience of a scientist (without
subject to his own interpretation via learning), adds the scientist's
bias to him which limits his creativity.  Setting a standard
for how knowledge is stored could severly limit these critters
in the same way msdos has severely limited personal computers.
The current best way we have of transmitting/storing knowledge,
the written word, seems remarkably versatile, and any scheme
that could replace it must be incredibly superior. I like the idea
of "skill chips" so that you can acquire expert skills in an area
temporarily if you need it, but there is big risk in assuming that
just because something claims to be an expert, that it is actually
best.

-RAY



