Comics are one of the best memetic vectors today.  We'd better not discount
the value an extropian comic book would have.

>...Comic superheros are very good at breaking ingenious constructions that
>their allegedly evil opponents have built.

Many of them are also good at building neat things, too, such as the afore-
mentioned Iron Man, Reed Richards of the Fantastic Four, Forge, the original
Guardian of Alpha Flight...

Most of the complaints about comics seem to be of the "they're not transhuman
enough" variety.  The problem with nigh-all-powerful characters is: what do
they do?  Galactus eats worlds, sort of a cosmic natural disaster.  That
gives the good guy something to fight against, but imagine a book starring him.
What could it consist of?  "Hmm.  Think I'll eat that world today.  Yum.  I
feel full now.  What's for desert?"  The "Secret Wars" and "Beyonder" series
proved the futility of trying to write stories for omnipotent, omniscient
beings.  What's the point of it all?  They know everything, they can do any-
thing, so why bother?

With characters with more human levels of power and vulnerabilities, on the
other hand, there's a whole lot more potential for exploring conflicts between
those powers and vulnerabilities as well as moral conflicts about how to use
them.  They are essentially like us, but a little stronger in some ways and
weaker in others.  As such, they're a hell of a lot more comprehensible to us
and more useful.

I actually found Vinge's "Fire Upon the Deep" flawed for the presense of all
the posthuman Powers.  Can you imagine a novel told from and starring Old One?
I can't.  Beyond a certain level, such characters become literally incomprehen-
sible except as hazards to be avoided like natural disasters.  It might be
nice to _be_ a posthuman or transhuman, but as long as we're mehums we're
inherently disadvantaged when it comes to transhuman stories.

The best comics are the ones whose characters are the most human, for they do
the best job of illustrating the possibilities of the human condition.  The
Dark Phoenix saga which has been attacked for being entropic was all an
illustration of Lord Acton's dictum: "Power tends to corrupt.  Absolute Power
corrupts absolutely."  The writer, Chris Claremont, gave a character absolute
power and showed how it could corrupt her - a cautionary tale.  But he also
showed how people could still take control of their destinies and make a
living while preventing others from coming to harm.  It was a tragedy, on a
literally cosmic scale.  Is all tragedy entropic?

Captain America, as written by Steve Ditko (himself a libertarian), has had
some of the most libertarian, patriotic silliloquys I've ever read.  The X-men
have had some of the best dialogues between Magneto as a support of benign
dictatorship versus the libertarian Xavier and other X-men I've ever read.
The whole notion of using mutants as a form of powerful minority persecuted
for accidents of its birth is a wonderful vehicle for the role of unique
individuals in society - outlaw, unsung heroes with the power to rule the world
if the chose to who choose not to because it's the wrong thing to do.  Instead,
they save it time and again because it's the right thing to do for themselves
and for the rest of the world, rarely getting thanked for their efforts.

Can you imagine a story in which a Jewish survivor of the Holocaust is now
trying to conquer the world to make it safe for Jews going up against a
onetime friend of his who knows you can't make anyone safer for long by means
of conquest?  Change "Jews" to "mutants," and you've got one of the running
themes of the X-men.  How about a story about the conflict between loyalty to
one's government and loyalty to one's country?  You'll find it in Captain
America.  And these are just two out of many, many examples.

Many comic books may be tame, entropic, unimaginative, etc., but many others
are among the best literature/entertainment we have.  Certainly at least as
good as the best science fiction novels, and leagues ahead of most of what
passes for serious literature today.

I'll propose a law of the economics of art: the more it costs to produce a
piece of art, the more entropic, boring, dull, unadventuresome, etc., it will
tend to be.  The cheaper it is, the more artistic risks will be taken, and
the more high rewards will sometimes be achieved.  Movies, by this standard,
cost a hell of a lot to make.  Music tapes & CDs cost a lot less.  Books are
one of the most expensive literary mediums, while comics are a lot cheaper.
Certainly, some mediums have advantages over others, but just compare Frank
Miller's "Batman: the Dark Knight Returns" to the Batman movie if you have any
doubt of my thesis.

