Now _that_ is an interesting point.  I think that this draws to 
a larger issue that is often overlooked, namely, does transhumanism
also imply a superior morality?  What happens when a transhuman
walks up to us and says, "X may seem immoral to you, but if you
could only see it from my perspective, it would all make sense."

At risk of appearing non-PC among the non-PC, I think that we must
ask the question of whether or not not Nazism might not fall under
the rubric of transhumanism; part of their agenda was to 
change humanity for the better through "advancing" morality
from the individual to the state, as well was trasformation
throught eugenic efforts.

I would also suggest that the "Nazi" issue might one major reason
that comic books are so culturally conservative--they were
borne, as I recall, around WWI and esp. WWII, with superheros
who embody "truth, justice, and [most of all] the American
Way," which is anti-elitist at heart (at least as far as our
fellow citizens are concerned).  Thus, comic books uphold the
notion--generally--of superheros protecting the commonweal,
including their less-powerful human neighbors.

           John Bozeman





